# Highly stable and efficient perovskite solar cells produced via high-boiling point solvents and additive engineering synergistically

1Key Laboratory of Applied Surface and Colloid Chemistry of the Ministry of Education, Shaanxi Engineering Lab for Advanced Energy Technology, School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Shaanxi Normal University, Xi’an 710062, China; $^2$ Key Laboratory of Chemical Reaction Engineering of Shaanxi Province, College of Chemistry & Chemical Engineering, Yan’an University, Yan’an 716000, China;   
3Dalian National Laboratory for Clean Energy, Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Dalian 116023, China;   
4Materials Science and Engineering, Penn State, University Park, PA, 16802, USA
The active absorber layer plays a crucial role in a perovskite solar cell. Herein, we used high boiling point $\gamma$ -butyrolactone (GBL) as the main solvent, ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) as an effective additive in the $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r_{{x}}}$ solution to improve the quality of perovskite films. The GBL will delay the crystallization speed of the perovskite, and lead to the grain growth assisted by thiocyanate. The synergistic effect of the solvent engineering and additive engineering is beneficial to the slow growth of the grain size. It is found that the addition of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ increases Gibbs free energy barrier for the nucleation, leading to the formation of fewer nuclei, which results in a high quality of perovskite absorbers with larger grains and smoother surfaces. The synergistic effect of solvents and $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ on the morphology and photovoltaic performances is investigated. Compared to devices without the additive, the efficiency of devices with $5\%$ $\mathrm{\}\%\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ -doped $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{MA}_{0.17}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{PbI}_{(3-x)}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ is raised to $19.01\%$ from $15.21\%$ . We believe this breakthrough regarding high efficiency perovskite solar cells will help for their transitions.
## perovskite, additive, crystallinity, grain size, efficiency
Citation: Wei Q, Ye Z, Ren X, Fu F, Yang Z, Liu SF, Yang D. Highly stable and efficient perovskite solar cells produced via high-boiling point solvents and additive engineering synergistically. Sc China Chem, 2020, 63, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-019-9727-8
## 1 Introduction
Hybrid organic-inorganic perovskite solar cells have experienced explosive developments, with the power conversion efficiency (PCE) rising from the initial value of $3\%$ to $25.2\%$ [1–15]. They are regarded as some of the most promising high efficiency solar cells, mainly due to their excellent photovoltaic properties, such as extremely high optical absorption coefficients, balanced carrier mobilities, long photogenerated carrier diffusion lengths, low defect densities, small exciton bonding energies [16–20]. The quality of the perovskite absorber is the key to high efficiency and stability of perovskite solar cells. To date, perovskite absorbers fabricated by the solution method have low crystallinity, awful surface coverage and poor stability, limiting the improvement of efficiency and stability of perovskite solar cells. This issue has become a bottleneck in the fabrication of high-performance perovskite solar cells.
There has been extensive research into increasing the efficiency and stability of perovskite solar cells through additive strategies and component engineering. Precursor solvent additives have been frequently used to improve the crystallinity, surface coverage and surface roughness of perovskite absorbers. All these results show that high boiling point solvents and additives can remarkably influence the grain size and crystallinity of the perovskite film. For example, Yang et al. [21] fabricated a $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ flexible perovskite solar cell with the highest PCE of $18.4\%$ by using a dimethyl sulfide additive. Jen et al. [22] employed 1,8- diiodooctane as an additive to obtain high-quality perovskite films. Park et al. [23] obtained highly crystalline, smoother and continuous perovskite films by simply adding dimethylsulfoxide into the precursor solution. Snaith et al. [24] utilized hydroiodic acid as an additive to grow a compact and uniform perovskite film. Grätzel et al. [25] used $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to obtain high-quality perovskite films, resulting in a high PCE of $18.0\%$ . Yan et al. [26,27] employed lead thiocyanate $(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2})$ in the perovskite precursor that can significantly increase the grain size and crystalline quality of perovskite thin films. The quality of perovskite absorbers has been enhanced through the incorporation of various additives. Solvent engineering and additive engineering were proposed in spin coating, where $N{\mathrm{,}}N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) and GBL were used as main solvents in the solution. In our work, with high-boiling GBL as the main solvents, ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ and DMSO as additives, in addition to the synergy of the solution and additive, high-quality perovskite films with the high coverage and large grain size is prepared. Solvent engineering and additive engineering was proposed in one-step spin coating, where ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ and DMSO were used as a cosolvent in the GBL solution. Therefore, it is important to further improve the efficiency of perovskite solar cells.
In this work, we present ternary-cation-component $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83^{-}}$ $\mathbf{MA}_{0.17}\mathbf{Cs}_{0.05}\mathbf{PbI}_{(3-x)}\mathbf{Br}_{x}$ perovskite films with the high crystallinity and large grain size through the use of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ and DMSO additives. Experimental results reveal that the synergistic effect of solvents and ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ not only improves the crystallinity but also promotes the coverage of perovskite films by forming chelates with lead iodide. The large grain size of the perovskite films decreases the number of defects at the grain boundaries, leading to high performances of the perovskite solar cells. It is found that the roughness of $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r_{{x}}}$ film is enhanced with increasing $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ concentrations, which is attributed to the large grain size when using the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive. We systematically study how the efficiency of planar $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{MA}_{0.17^{-}}$ $\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{PbI}_{(3-x)}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ solar cells is changed with different ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ contents, and the champion efficiency is as high as $19.01\%$ when using $5\%\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additives, which is approximately $25\%$ higher than that for a device without the additive.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials and reagents
Lead iodide $(\mathrm{Pb}_{2}$ , $99\%$ ), ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ , lithium bis(trifluoromethylsulphonyl) imide (Li-TFSI, $99.95\%$ ), 4-tertbutylpyridine (tBP, $96\%$ ), and chlorobenzene $(99.8\%)$ were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Hydroiodic acid $(57~\mathrm{wt\%})$ and formamidine acetate $(99\%)$ were bought from the Tianjin Kermol Chemical Regent Developing Center. 2,2,7,7-Tetrakis-(N,N-di- $p$ -methoxyphenyl-amine)-9,9-spirobiuorene (spiro-MeOTAD) was obtained from YingkouYouxuan Technology Company (China). Lead(II) iodide $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , $99.9985\%$ , lead(II) bromine $({\mathrm{Pb}}\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ , $99.9985\%$ ) and CsI $(99.9\%)$ were purchased from Alfa Aesar (USA). Fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) glass from Wuhan Geao Company (China). All materials were directly used without any purification.
## 2.2 Material synthesis and solar cell fabrication
## 2.3 Characterizations
X-ray diffractometer (XRD, DX-2700) measurements were recorded using a Rikagu diffractometer ( $\mathrm{CuK}\mathfrak{a}$ radiation, $\lambda=$ $0.15406\mathrm{nm})$ running at $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $30\mathrm{mA}$ . The absorption spectra of $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}}$ films on the different substrates were obtained using a PerkinElmer spectrophotometer (Lambda 950 UV-vis-NIR, USA) in the wavelength ranging from 300 to $850\mathrm{nm}$ . Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were acquired using a JEOL JSM-7610F (Japan). The purity and the chemical composition of the products were analyzed by energy dispersive spectrometry (EDS). The height atomic force microscope (AFM) images were taken on a Bruker Metrology Nanoscope VIII AFM in ambient (Germany). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed by an ESCALAB 250 instrument using a source of Al $\mathrm{Ka}$ radiation.The photocurrent density-voltage $(J{-}V)$ curves of the solar cells were measured at room temperature under AM 1.5 G, $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ light illumination, which is generated by a sunlight simulator (Keithely 2400 source meter) and calibrated by a standard Si solar cell. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) measurements were carried out by QTest Station 2000ADI system (Crowntech Inc., USA) in the AC mode.
## 3 Results and discussion
Crystalline $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r}_{x}$ perovskite films were prepared on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -coated FTO substrates. Figure 1(a) shows the XRD patterns of $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{MA}_{0.17}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{PbI}_{(3-x)}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ films with various $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ contents. All the films show major diffraction peaks of the perovskite, namely the dominant peaks located at $14.08^{\circ}$ , $28.40^{\circ}$ , and $31.86^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (110), (220), and (310) lattice planes, respectively [28–30]. When the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ content reaches $7.5\%$ , a $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak is clearly observed in the XRD pattern. We propose that the passivation of remnant $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is associated with its location, which is controlled by the deposition methods and identified through XRD and SEM [31]. Figure 1(b) shows that the intensities of all the characteristic peaks from the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ -doped film are obviously increased compared to those of $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r_{\it x}},$ demonstrating the significantly improved crystallinity. The degree of crystallinity of the perovskite calculated from the full width at half maxima (FWHM) and intensities of the (110) peaks are listed in Table S1 (Supporting Information online).
The $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r_{\it x}}$ film fabricated with $7.5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ provided the highest peak intensity and the smallest peak width of 0.30, indicating the better crystallinity compared to the pristine perovskite films with the peak width of 0.36. However, when the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ concentration is less than $5\%$ , the samples have lower peak intensities and wider peaks. The large grain size and good crystallinity are important to enhance the charge transport in perovskite solar cells.
Figure 2(a, b) show the UV-Vis absorption spectra of a series of perovskite $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{MA}_{0.17}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{PbI}_{(3-x)}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ films. The absorption edge extends over the complete visible spectrum, with two distinct peaks located near 480 and $780\mathrm{nm}$ . The $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ for $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r_{{x}}}$ perovskite films are estimated to be ${\sim}1.59\mathrm{eV},$ , in good agreement with the literatures [27,32]. The absorption peak intensity exhibits a rising tendency with increasing ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ contents in the wavelength range from 550 to $780\mathrm{nm}$ , but when the content of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ exceeds $5\%$ , the absorption intensity decreases immediately. The samples with the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ doping show significantly higher absorbance in the wavelength range from 700 to $780\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Figure 2(b), although the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additives in the precursors do not change the bandgap of the perovskite absorbers, the excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at grain boundaries can reduce the absorbance of the perovskite films, which is not desirable for photovoltaic performances. This result is consistent with the photoelectric performance test, which will be discussed below.
Figure 3(a–e) and Figure S1 (Supporting Information online) show top-view SEM images of the $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{MA}_{0.17}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}-$ $\mathrm{PbI}_{(3-x)}$ films prepared with different ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ contents. It is clear that the $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r_{\mathrm{{x}}}}$ films with $\mathrm{Pb}$ $(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ added into the precursor solution display the better surface coverage. The average grain size has no obvious change with increasing the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ content from $1.5\%$ to $2.5\%$ ; however, when the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ is increased to $5\%$ , the grain size of perovskite films have enlarged quite obviously, and the large grain size would increase the carrier mobility within the perovskite film and reduce the recombination. The large grain size and good crystalline quality are key factors for improving the performance of perovskite solar cells. Figure 3(f) shows the cross-sectional SEM image of a complete device with $5\%$ $/_{0}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive with a layered structure of FTO $(300\mathrm{nm})$ , compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(50\mathrm{nm})$ , $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83^{-}}$ $\mathbf{MA}_{0.17}\mathbf{Cs}_{0.05}\mathbf{PbI}_{(3-x)}\mathbf{Br}_{x}$ $(400\mathrm{nm})$ , spiro-OMeTAD $(200\mathrm{nm})$ , and gold $(80\mathrm{nm})$ . In the figure, the interfaces are clearly visible, and perovskite crystallites are large and run throughout the entire section. There are two main advantages when using the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive: i) The $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ can form a stable intermediate with the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , thus slowing the crystallization process. ii) The use of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ as a functional additive would mainly increase the grain size during the heating and annealing process. In addition, the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive would lead to excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in the perovskite film, which could result in the significant passivation both at the grain boundaries and at the top and bottom of the film [31,32]. It is worth noting that the synergistic effect of ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ and DMSO additives significantly increases the perovskite crystallites. DMSO and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ can form a complex intermediate, which would generate the ammonia due to the decomposition of ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ during annealing process, promoting the growth of the high quality of films. The GBL and DMSO solvents inhibit the excessive growth of lead iodide at the grain boundary, leading to the formation of small amount of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at the grain boundary. When $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ content is greatly increased, the grain size of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ will also increase significantly between the perovskite grains. Therefore, a high quality perovskite film is obtained through the synergy between solvents and additive engineering. As shown in Figure 4(a, b), the crystal sizes of the perovskite films with $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ significantly increase to $500{\mathrm{-}}1000\ \mathrm{nm}$ , while the grain sizes of pristine perovskite films are only $50{-}150~\mathrm{nm}$ . The AFM images of the $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r}_{x}$ perovskite films are shown in Figure 4(c, d). The surface roughnessis 17.65 and $7.43\mathrm{nm}$ for pristine and $5\%$ $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ -doped perovskite films, respectively. The large grain size would serve to increase the carrier mobility and reduce the recombination at grain boundaries. Figure 5 shows C, N, I, Br, Cs and $\mathrm{Pb}$ mapping images of the perovskite films with $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ . These results well prove the composition of perovskite films, suggesting that all elements are uniformly incorporated into the $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{MA}_{0.17}$ $\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{PbI}_{(3-x)}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ films. Above results indicate that we have obtained the high-quality perovskite films with the combination of encouraging characteristics including full surface coverage, a large grain size, long-range continuous and homogeneous thin-film morphology and a high level of crystallization phase purity. The high quality of the perovskite absorber layers plays a key role in determining the performance of the perovskite solar cells.
The results of XPS are shown in Figure 6. The $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83^{-}}$ $\mathbf{MA}_{0.17}\mathbf{Cs}_{0.05}\mathbf{Pb}\mathbf{I}_{(3-x)}\mathbf{Br}_{x}$ has two peaks assigned to $\mathbf{Pb}_{4\mathrm{f}7/2}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}_{4\mathrm{f}5/2}$ , which are located at 137.1 and $141.9\mathrm{eV},$ respectively. The $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{MA}_{0.17}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{PbI}_{(3-x)}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ pervoskite film with $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ shows strong signals for I 3d and $\mathrm{Cs}3\mathrm{d}$ core levels. The C 1s peak of the perovskite film is located at $286.02\mathrm{eV}.$ . The XPS spectrum of a perovskite film with $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive does not show any evidence of S because the accompanying formation of HSCN gas leads to the loss of most SCN ions from the films [26,27,33]. The above results demonstrate that the $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive in the perovskite precursor would improve the crystalline quality and result in the formation of excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at the grain boundaries. This excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at the grain boundaries could passivate grain boundaries and exhibit beneficial effects on device performances [34,35]. According to Yan’s research, the formation of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ gas during the annealing process could be responsible for the enlarged grain size and improved crystalline quality of the $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r_{{x}}}$ perovskite films, and the accompanying formation of HSCN gas leads to the loss of most SCN from the films. The ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additives in the precursors would only improve the crystalline quality (Figure S1). Some literatures have reported the addition of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additives to increase the size of the grains and improve the film quality. While we used GBL and DMSO as a mixed solvent to form a high quality perovskite film, a large amount of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is not formed at the grain boundary and the film surface, but a larger crystal grains are formed. There are mainly three advantages to with ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ and DMSO additives during the perovskite crystallization process. i) The high-boiling point solvent (GBL) retards the crystallization speed of the perovskite to produce large grain size. GBL $(204^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ has a higher boiling point than DMF $(152.8^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , which can effectively promote perovskite and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ growth. ii) The sulfur atoms of DMSO are more capable of providing electrons to coordinate with the empty $\mathrm{Pb}5\mathrm{d}$ orbitals to form a hexacoordinated complex, leading to more stable intermediates. iii) The use of ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ as a functional additive can mainly adjust the grain size. It would generate $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ during the annealing process, which can assist the growth of perovskite grains to increase the grain size in perovskite films [36,37]. For DMSO, according to Park’s research, when DMSO was introduced into the perovskite precursor solution, it would chelates with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ to form an intermediate complex, resulting in the slow growth speed for the perovskite crystallization because DMSO could be slowly removed during the annealing process.
Figure 8 shows the steady state photoluminescence (PL) spectra of $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/F A_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r_{{x}}}$ films with and without ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ . The PL intensity is sharply enhanced with increasing ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ concentrations, indicating significantly reduced carrier recombinations, which is beneficial to the efficient charge extraction. Although the perovskite film with $7.5\%\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ displays the highest PL intensity, and thus the lowest carrier recombination, the large amount of ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ would produce too many $\mathrm{Pb}$ ions, resulting in poor performances of perovskite solar cells (Figure 7(a) and Table S2). Thus, $5\%$ $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive could produce the highest PCE for perovskite devices, in excellent accordance with the $J_{-}V$ results.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we have demonstrated a facile one-step solution method for the fabrication of high-quality $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{MA}_{0.17^{-}}$ $\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{PbI}_{(3-x)}\mathrm{Br}_{x}$ perovskite films. The point is the addition of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ and DMSO into the perovskite precursor solution, which affects the crystallization process and results in the high quality of the $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r}_{x}$ films with large grain sizes and full surface coverages, leading to reduced charge recombinations. The perovskite solar cells with ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive exhibit the high efficiency, good reproducibility, and excellent stability. Compared with the pristine $\mathrm{FA_{0.83}M A_{0.17}C s_{0.05}P b I_{(3-x)}B r}_{x}$ control device, the champion efficiency of a device with $5\%$ $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ is increased to $19.01\%$ from $15.21\%$ . We believe that this convenient additive technology will open up an effective route for improving the performances of perovskite solar cells.
